# CODEOWNERS — whom GitHub requests for review when a matching path changes.
#
# There is one maintainer today, so every line points to the same handle. The value right
# now is the automatic review request and a written record of who answers for what; as the
# project grows, the per-path lines are where a chapter's owner is named without handing
# them the whole tree. Last matching pattern wins, so the specific blocks below override
# the default.
#
# NOTE: this file only *assigns* reviewers — it does not *require* them. Requiring
# code-owner approval is a separate ruleset switch, and turning it on while there is a
# single owner would lock that owner out of merging their own PRs. Leave it off until a
# second maintainer exists.

# Default owner for everything not matched below.
*                       @haed

# Governance and licensing answer for the project as a whole and stay with the maintainer
# even once chapter owners exist. Listed explicitly so a later, narrower rule never
# quietly takes them.
/LICENSE                @haed
/LICENSE-CODE           @haed
/NOTICE                 @haed
/GOVERNANCE.md          @haed

# CI and this file itself — the keys to the kingdom. A change here can weaken every other
# guard.
/.github/               @haed

# The normative text. Every requirement here is something a third party may already have
# implemented and tested against, so a change is never routine — an ID that moves is a
# silent break in somebody else's conformance report.
/spec/                  @haed

# The authoring rules that keep the above true.
/docs/agents/           @haed
